"We respect boundaries," the Roots explained to younger plants. "The Stone has its realm, we have ours."

But the surface soil was thin and poor. Roots found little nourishment in the shallow earth. Plants grew weak and toppled in mild winds. The soil itself began washing away—nothing held it firmly in place.

Deep below, the Stone remained solid and unchanged, but also sterile. Minerals stayed locked inside. No channels formed for water to flow. The earth became dense and lifeless.

One day, a wild Root abandoned politeness and drove downward, pressing into every crack in the Stone. The Stone resisted, splitting the Root, forcing it to adapt. The Root persisted, fracturing the Stone bit by bit.

Their struggle seemed violent, but from it came transformation. The Stone's minerals broke free, enriching the earth. The Root's pressure created channels where water could move. Broken stone and broken root together became rich soil.

"We thought we were fighting," the Stone realized.

"We were collaborating," the Root replied, "in the only honest way we knew."
